{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"S-near-1:2\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The name invoked in 1:1 is immediately the recipient of praise in 1:2 and is named as Lord of the worlds; the opening moves from invocation into praise of that same source.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same divine name occurs across the two opening ayat, while praise and lordship follow the mercy epithets.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence connects invocation and praise but does not specify an omitted verb in 1:1.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-near-1:3\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The exact pair ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ returns after the praise and lordship of 1:2, making mercy a recurrent frame in the opening rather than a one-time close to the invocation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:3\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The paired forms recur verbatim as a separate ayah after an intervening description of Allah.\",\"limits\":\"The recurrence establishes renewed emphasis but does not by itself assign distinct meanings to the two forms.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-near-1:4\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The merciful names are followed by ownership of the Day of Recompense, placing mercy and final judgment within the same portrait of God.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The sequence moves from the repeated mercy pair in 1:3 to the title and day in 1:4.\",\"limits\":\"The adjacency holds mercy and judgment together; it does not say that either cancels or explains the other.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-near-1:5\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The opening's named source becomes the addressee of the first-person community's explicit acts: worship and asking for aid. This gives the invocation a relational continuation in prayer.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The surah shifts from naming Allah to addressing the singular ٱللَّهِيَّاكَ as the one worshipped and asked for aid.\",\"limits\":\"The later prayer does not identify the precise unspoken action governed by بِسْمِ in 1:1.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-near-1:6\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The address to the merciful source leads into a request for guidance along a straight path; the opening prayer thus proceeds from naming to asking for direction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The guidance request follows the invocation and the direct address of 1:5.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence does not say that the name itself is a road sign or that guidance is continuous correction.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-near-1:7\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The requested straight path is then described through benefaction and two exclusions, placing the opening's mercy before a route with specified outcomes and boundaries.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"صِرَٰطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The route requested in 1:6 is specified in the final ayah by those favored and those characterized by anger or straying.\",\"limits\":\"This sequence does not lexically define mercy as a boundary or equate the named groups with fixed classes of people.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-root-ءله\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"same-as:S-near-1:2\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-root-رحم\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"same-as:S-near-1:3\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-baseline_operative_designation\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The opening leaves an act unstated, and the next ayah begins the surah's explicit speech with praise of Allah. Praise is the first act that follows the invocation, though the text does not make it the missing verb.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The opening prepositional phrase is followed by a new ayah whose first statement praises the same named source.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence does not establish that 1:2 completes a grammatical ellipsis in 1:1 or that every act under the name has this form.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-baseline_legible_mercy_mark\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The invocation might be heard as marking an act whose effects should make its merciful affiliation recognizable; 1:2 supplies praise and lordship, but no visible effect of an act.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The surah does not describe an act or its observable result as a sign of the name. A dictionary or Quranic usage showing س م و as an identifying, readable mark, together with an ayah linking such a mark to an act's effects, could supply that bridge.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-baseline_generative_enclosure\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The repeated mercy root could support hearing the invocation as a generative, protected relation from which an unnamed act emerges.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:3\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"}],\"missing\":\"These ayat contain no birth, womb, cover, or kinship language. Dictionary evidence for those senses of ر ح م and Quranic usage that activates them in these mercy forms could establish the proposed enclosure.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-delta_1_2_cultivated_public_proof\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The praise and lordship in 1:2 might be heard as a public result by which the opening's claimed merciful affiliation can be recognized.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The text gives praise and lordship but does not describe cultivation, repair, or a completed benefit as evidence for the invocation. An ayah connecting divine lordship to a discernible beneficial result, or lexical evidence for the proposed identifying-mark sense, could supply this link.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-delta_1_2_rain_womb_cultivation\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"A rain-and-growth reading would connect the root associations proposed for ٱسْم, the mercy forms, and رَبِّ into a sequence of canopy, enclosure, and emergence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"Neither ayah names rain, clouds, earth, growth, womb, or emergence. Dictionary or Quranic parallels that connect these specific forms and roots in such a sequence could provide the missing support.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-delta_1_3_mercy_as_recurring_field\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The full mercy pair returns after 1:2's praise and lordship, so the opening's mercy continues across a change in what is said about God instead of ending with 1:1.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"ٱلْحَمْدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلْعَٰلَمِينَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:3\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The same two definite forms occupy both 1:1 and the whole of 1:3, with praise and lordship between them.\",\"limits\":\"The recurrence supports continuity of emphasis but does not establish that one form means generative containment and the other ongoing care.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-delta_1_4_accountable_mercy\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The surah keeps tenderness beside sovereignty over the Day of Recompense: the opening's mercy belongs to the same portrait as consequential account.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The mercy pair is repeated in 1:3 and immediately followed by the title and day of 1:4.\",\"limits\":\"This arrangement gives mercy a judgment horizon but does not specify how mercy affects the judgment or its outcome.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-delta_1_4_account_bearing_seal\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The invocation might be heard as attributing an act to the named sovereign and binding it to an eventual settlement, linking designation with ownership and account.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:4\",\"ar\":\"مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The surah puts the name and the Day of Recompense in one portrait but does not call the invocation a seal, deed, or liability record. Lexical evidence for a mark sense of س م و and debt or obligation senses of د ي ن, plus Quranic usage linking a named act to later account, could establish that model.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-delta_1_5_yielded_and_enabled_agency\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The prayer's two verbs form a circuit: the speakers yield themselves in worship and ask the same addressee for aid. This gives the opening's name an agency frame of devotion and dependence.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The named divine source of the opening is followed by exclusive address, worship, and a request for help.\",\"limits\":\"The prayer supports devotion and dependence but does not state that the speakers' agency is literally transferred and returned.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-delta_1_5_attentive_custody\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The mercy invocation before the request for aid could invite a reading of help as attentive, sustaining custody rather than a single intervention.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"وَإِيَّاكَ نَسْتَعِينُ\"}],\"missing\":\"The surah does not say that aid is watchfulness, preservation, or development. A dictionary or Quranic usage establishing the proposed eye or custody sense for ع و ن, and a contextual cue connecting it to womb-mercy, could supply this.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-delta_1_6_name_as_wayfinding_heading\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The movement from the named source to the request for a straight path permits the invocation to be heard as a heading for the journey that follows.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The surah moves from divine naming through direct address to a request for guidance and a traversable path.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence does not say that ٱسْم is a navigational sign or that the heading continuously corrects the travelers.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-delta_1_6_guidance_as_relational_gift\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"Because the guidance request follows the merciful names and directly addresses God, guidance could be heard as care received within a sustaining relation rather than route information alone.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The request does not call guidance a gift or say that it preserves the travelers in alignment. Quranic usage or lexical evidence connecting ه د ي to a bestowed gift and ق و م to active preservation could establish those added senses.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-delta_1_7_discriminating_mercy_boundary\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The path placed after the mercy invocation is described by favor and bounded by anger and straying; the surah presents the requested trajectory with discernible inclusions and exclusions.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"صِرَٰطَ ٱلَّذِينَ أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The final ayah specifies the straight path immediately after it is requested, naming favor and two opposed conditions.\",\"limits\":\"The sequence supports a discriminating route but does not establish that the root of ٱسْم means an affiliation boundary or that the excluded descriptions are permanent identities.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-delta_1_7_mercy_as_plastic_reformation\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The path's straightness and the contrasts of favor, anger, and straying could support a model in which mercy makes a person formable toward balance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"أَنْعَمْتَ عَلَيْهِمْ غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"These ayat describe a path and conditions but do not mention softness, material pliability, changing form, or rock-hardness. Lexical and Quranic evidence for those associations in the cited roots, plus a passage describing their transformation, could supply the bridge.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-outlier_narrow_swallowing_gate\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The sequence from invocation to the requested path could be explored as entry into a divinely directed way that displaces self-authored direction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا ٱلصِّرَٰطَ ٱلْمُسْتَقِيمَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The surah contains no explicit gate, aperture, swallowing, or disappearance. Lexical and Quranic usage establishing those senses for the proposed roots, and a cue tying them to this invocation and path, could support the stronger image.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-outlier_guiding_lullaby\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The paired cadence of the mercy names, repeated in 1:3 before the guidance request, could be explored as settling the reader into receptivity to guidance.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:3\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:6\",\"ar\":\"ٱهْدِنَا\"}],\"missing\":\"Repetition provides a formal cue but does not establish soothing, child-sleep, or composure. Recitational evidence about the cadence or Quranic usage of ه د ي in a soothing sense could supply that support.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-outlier_anti_loss_recovery_mark\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The opening name might be explored as a recoverable identity or direction mark set against the straying named at the surah's close.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"وَلَا ٱلضَّآلِّينَ\"}],\"missing\":\"The surah juxtaposes naming and straying but does not say that the name is a sign used to recover lost direction. Lexical or Quranic evidence for the proposed identifying-mark sense, and a passage connecting it to recovery from straying, could supply that link.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"S-hft-outlier_interior_reconciliation\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The mercy names and the final ayah's language of difference could be explored as a kin-making relation that reconciles separated parties without erasing their distinction.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱلرَّحْمَٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:7\",\"ar\":\"غَيْرِ ٱلْمَغْضُوبِ عَلَيْهِمْ\"}],\"missing\":\"The surah names difference and mercy but contains no act of reconciliation or rejoining. Lexical or Quranic evidence for the proposed inner-circle and kinship senses, along with a passage showing them reconciling separated parties, could supply it.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The opening moves from naming Allah to praising Him, then the surah shifts into direct second-person address at 1:5; its progression turns divine designation into spoken relation.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"1:1\",\"ar\":\"ٱللَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:2\",\"ar\":\"لِلَّهِ\"},{\"ref\":\"1:5\",\"ar\":\"إِيَّاكَ نَعْبُدُ\"}],\"activation\":\"The first two ayat speak of Allah by name, while 1:5 addresses Him as ٱلْمُخَاطَبُ through إِيَّاكَ.\",\"limits\":\"This pronoun shift establishes a change in address but does not by itself establish an unspoken divine response or a particular emotional effect.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}"}}
